statement:
For a natural number $n$, let $\mathrm{equality}_n$ denote the two-party Boolean function on $n$-bit strings that returns $\mathtt{true}$ precisely when Alice's input $x \in \mathrm{BoolInput}(n)$ equals Bob's input $y \in \mathrm{BoolInput}(n)$. Its deterministic communication complexity, measured in $\bbn_\infty$ as the least worst-case number of bits exchanged by any deterministic protocol computing it, is
\[
  D(\mathrm{equality}_n) \;=\;
  \begin{cases}
    0 & \text{if } n = 0,\\
    n + 1 & \text{otherwise.}
  \end{cases}
\]
